  <h2 id="scope">1. Scope of this policy</h2>
  <p>
    Big Tree Services ("we", "us", "our") provides a web-based CRM that arborist and tree care
    companies use to manage estimates, quotes, scheduling, invoicing and field work. This policy
    explains what information the application collects, how we use it, who we share it with and
    what rights you have.
  </p>
  <p>
    It covers the application and these public pages. It does not cover any third-party website
    or service we link to, which is governed by that party's own policy.
  </p>

  <h2 id="roles">2. Our role and yours</h2>
  <p>
    The application is sold to businesses, and each subscribing company is a separate tenant.
    In respect of the business records a company puts into the application &mdash; its customer
    list, quotes, invoices, job records and so on &mdash; the <strong>company is the controller
    of that data and we act as its processor</strong>. We handle that data on the company's
    instructions and for the purpose of operating the service for them.
  </p>
  <p>
    In respect of staff account information and technical logs, and of these public pages, we act
    as the controller.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you are a customer of a tree care company that uses our application and you want your
    details corrected or removed, please contact that company directly; we will assist them in
    responding. See <a href="#customers">section 11</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="collect">3. Information we collect</h2>

  <h3>3.1 Staff account information</h3>
  <p>
    When a company creates an account for a member of staff, we collect that person's name, email
    address, phone number, job role and permission level, a hashed password, and &mdash; where the
    company uses these features &mdash; employment details such as pay rate, certifications and
    licence expiry dates uploaded for compliance tracking.
  </p>

  <h3>3.2 Business data entered into the application</h3>
  <p>
    Staff users enter the records needed to run the business. This typically includes:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>customer names, addresses, email addresses and phone numbers;</li>
    <li>property and site details, including the location of work;</li>
    <li>tree inventories &mdash; species, measurements, condition notes and planned work actions;</li>
    <li>photographs of trees, sites and completed work;</li>
    <li>estimates, costings, quotes, invoices, payments and receipts;</li>
    <li>crew hours, equipment assignments, expense receipts and internal notes.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We do not ask for and do not want special categories of data (such as health information) or
    payment card numbers to be entered into free-text fields.
  </p>

  <h3>3.3 Connected mailbox data</h3>
  <p>
    If a company administrator connects a Google Workspace or Microsoft 365 mailbox, we receive an
    OAuth authorisation and the connected email address. See
    <a href="#google">section 4</a>, which sets out exactly what we do and do not access.
  </p>

  <h3>3.4 Technical and usage data</h3>
  <p>
    Our servers record standard information about requests: IP address, browser and device type,
    pages requested, timestamps and referring page. We use a cookie to keep you signed in, and we
    store your selected organisation in your browser's local storage. We use
    <strong>Sentry</strong> to capture error reports when something goes wrong, which may include
    the URL, the signed-in user's identifier and a technical stack trace. These public pages do not
    use advertising or tracking cookies.
  </p>

  <h2 id="google">4. Google and Microsoft user data</h2>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      Big Tree Services' use and transfer of information received from Google APIs to any other app
      will adhere to the
      <a href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Google
      API Services User Data Policy</a>, including the Limited Use requirements. We apply the same
      standard to data received from Microsoft Graph.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3>4.1 Why we ask for access</h3>
  <p>
    Quotes, invoices, receipts and appointment notices need to reach the customer from the tree care
    company's own business address. A company administrator therefore connects the company mailbox
    so that the application can send those documents on the company's behalf.
  </p>

  <h3>4.2 What we access</h3>
  <p>
    We request only the permission needed to <strong>send mail on the connected account's behalf</strong>,
    together with the basic account detail (the email address) needed to display and validate the
    connection. The connection is made by OAuth through our email infrastructure provider, Nylas.
    We never see, request or store the mailbox password.
  </p>

  <h3>4.3 What we do not access</h3>
  <p>
    We do <strong>not</strong> request or use permission to read, search, download, modify or delete
    existing messages in the mailbox, and we do not access contacts, calendars, files or any other
    Google or Microsoft service.
  </p>

  <h3>4.4 What we retain</h3>
  <p>
    For each message the application sends we store a delivery record consisting of the recipient
    address, the time sent, the type of document sent, the staff user who sent it, the related job
    record and the provider's message identifier. We store the message subject and body only as part
    of the job record the message was generated from. We store the OAuth grant identifier, the
    connected email address and the connection status.
  </p>

  <h3>4.5 Limits on our use</h3>
  <p>
    Data obtained through Google or Microsoft APIs is used <strong>only to provide and improve the
    sending feature described above</strong>. We do not use it for advertising, we do not sell or
    transfer it, we do not use it to train generalised artificial intelligence or machine learning
    models, and no human at Big Tree Services reads it except where you have specifically asked us
    to (for example to resolve a support ticket), where it is necessary for security purposes or to
    comply with applicable law, or where the data is aggregated and anonymised.
  </p>

  <h3>4.6 Revoking access</h3>
  <p>
    An administrator can disconnect the mailbox at any time from the company settings page in the
    application, which revokes our authorisation immediately. Access can also be revoked from
    <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/permissions" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Google Account
    permissions</a> or the equivalent Microsoft account page. On disconnection we delete the stored
    grant; historical delivery records remain on the related job records as part of the company's
    own business history.
  </p>

  <h2 id="use">5. How we use information</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>to provide the application and its features to the company that subscribes to it;</li>
    <li>to authenticate users and enforce the permissions their company has assigned them;</li>
    <li>to generate and send quotes, invoices, receipts and notifications on the company's behalf;</li>
    <li>to produce reports and exports the company requests;</li>
    <li>to provide support, diagnose faults and keep the service secure and available;</li>
    <li>to bill for the service and keep the records required by law;</li>
    <li>to comply with legal obligations and to establish or defend legal claims.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We do not sell personal information, we do not share it for behavioural advertising, and we do
    not use customer business data to train artificial intelligence models.
  </p>

  <h2 id="share">6. Sharing and service providers</h2>
  <p>
    We share information only as follows.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>With the subscribing company.</strong> Staff of a company can see the data belonging
      to that company, subject to the permissions their administrator sets.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>With service providers</strong> who process data on our behalf under contract:
      <em>Nylas</em> (email delivery and OAuth connection management), <em>Amazon Web Services</em>
      (application hosting and file storage for uploaded photographs and documents), and
      <em>Sentry</em> (error monitoring). Each is permitted to use the data only to provide its
      service to us.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>With recipients you direct us to email</strong> &mdash; the customers and staff a
      company sends quotes, invoices and notifications to.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Where the law requires it</strong>, in response to a valid legal process, or where
      necessary to protect our rights, our users or the public.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>In a business transfer.</strong> If our business is sold or reorganised, information
      may transfer with it, remaining subject to a policy no less protective than this one.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="location">7. Where data is held</h2>
  <p>
    The application and its database are hosted in North America. Uploaded photographs and documents
    are stored in Amazon S3. Our service providers may process data in the United States and other
    countries, which means information may be transferred outside your province or country and may
    be accessible to the courts and authorities of those jurisdictions in accordance with their law.
    We use contractual protections with our providers for these transfers.
  </p>

  <h2 id="retention">8. Retention</h2>
  <p>
    We retain a company's business data for as long as its account is active. Following termination
    we retain it for <strong>90 days</strong> so it can be exported, after which we delete or
    anonymise it, except where we are required to keep records for longer for tax, accounting or
    legal reasons. Staff account records are deleted or anonymised when the account is removed and
    the retention period has passed. Error reports and server logs are kept for up to 90 days.
  </p>

  <h2 id="security">9. Security</h2>
  <p>
    We protect information with measures appropriate to its sensitivity: encryption in transit
    (HTTPS), hashed passwords, role-based access controls, tenant isolation so one company cannot
    see another's data, restricted administrative access, and monitoring. No system is perfectly
    secure, and we cannot guarantee absolute security. If a breach occurs that creates a real risk
    of significant harm, we will notify the affected companies and the relevant regulator as the
    law requires.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rights">10. Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    Subject to applicable law, you may ask us to give you access to the personal information we hold
    about you, correct it if it is inaccurate, delete it, restrict or object to a particular use, or
    provide it in a portable format. You may also withdraw a consent you have given, though this may
    mean we can no longer provide part of the service.
  </p>
  <p>
    Email <a href="mailto:info@bigtreeservices.ca">info@bigtreeservices.ca</a> to make a request. We
    will respond within 30 days and may need to verify your identity first. Where we hold your
    information as a processor for a subscribing company, we will refer your request to that company
    and support them in answering it.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you are not satisfied with our response, you may complain to the Office of the Privacy
    Commissioner of Canada or to your local data protection authority.
  </p>

  <h2 id="customers">11. Customers of our users</h2>
  <p>
    If you received a quote, invoice or appointment notice generated by this application, your
    details were entered by the tree care company you dealt with. That company decides what it
    records about you and for how long. Please direct any request about your details to that
    company; its contact information appears on the document you received.
  </p>

  <h2 id="children">12. Children</h2>
  <p>
    The application is a workplace tool intended for adults. It is not directed at children and we do
    not knowingly collect personal information from anyone under 16. If we learn that we have, we
    will delete it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="changes">13. Changes</h2>
  <p>
    We may update this policy from time to time. The effective date at the top shows when it last
    changed. If a change materially affects how we handle personal information we will give notice
    in the application or by email before it takes effect.
  </p>

  <h2 id="contact">14. Contact</h2>
  <p>
